    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a takeout classic. One order of moo shu at a restaurant costs more than this entire batch of fourteen containers.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCabbage is the reason this is cheap.\u003c/strong\u003e One head fills the pan and costs less than almost anything else in produce. It also keeps for weeks, unlike the bagged slaw mixes.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eHoisin and oyster sauce are one time buys.\u003c/strong\u003e Both bottles last for many batches and both live in the fridge for months. They are what make this taste like the restaurant version.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy a whole pork shoulder and slice it yourself.\u003c/strong\u003e Pre sliced stir fry pork carries a real markup for knife work you can do in ten minutes. Freeze it for twenty minutes first and it slices thin easily.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eSesame oil goes in off the heat.\u003c/strong\u003e It burns fast and it is one of the pricier bottles in the pantry. A small amount at the end flavors the whole batch."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Slice the pork shoulder thin against the grain and toss it with some of the soy sauce, the cornstarch and the black pepper. Let it sit while you prep everything else.",
                    "Shred the cabbage, cut the carrots into matchsticks, slice the mushrooms, mince the garlic, and separate the green onion whites from the tops.",
                    "Beat the eggs and scramble them in a little of the vegetable oil until just set, then take them out and chop them roughly.",
                    "Get your largest pan very hot, add more oil, and stir fry the marinated pork in batches until it is browned and cooked through. Crowding the pan will steam the pork instead of searing it.",
                    "Add the garlic and the green onion whites, then the mushrooms, carrots and cabbage, and stir fry until the cabbage wilts but still has some bite left in it, seasoning with a little salt.",
                    "Return the pork, add the hoisin sauce, oyster sauce, the rest of the soy sauce and the chicken broth, and toss until everything is glazed.",
                    "Kill the heat, fold in the scrambled egg and the green onion tops, finish with the sesame oil, and serve over rice."
                ],
